WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.140
- [Devin] Life in prison
for throwing a glitter bomb.

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:04.870
- Wait, what?

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:07.090
I thought you said this was legal.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.880
- No, Mark Rober is
probably not going to jail,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:12.840
but two Florida women have been
charged with felony offenses

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.330
for allegedly throwing a glitter bomb

00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:16.610
at a man inside an apartment complex.

00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:18.610
Their arrest made headlines
with some announcing

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:22.510
that they faced life in
prison for a glitter bomb.

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:24.230
Now while it's theoretically possible

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.830
that they could face
that kind of a sentence,

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.890
they probably won't
face anything like that,

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.740
but their actions do go well
beyond putting some glitter

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.560
in an envelope and surprising
some porch pirates.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:36.430
So they do face potential heavy jail time.

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:38.040
And all of this has Twitter wondering

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.540
just what are the legal implications

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.960
of attacking people with glitter.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:42.950
Is it illegal to send
someone a box of glitter

00:00:42.950 --> 00:00:43.783
through the mail?

00:00:43.783 --> 00:00:45.170
Can you deal with a would-be thief

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:46.870
by booby trapping a package?

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.670
Just how much money does Hobby
Lobby make in glitter sales?

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.710
And how do we stop Mark Rober?

00:00:52.710 --> 00:00:54.943
Today we're talking
everything glitter bombs.

00:00:54.943 --> 00:00:57.610
(upbeat music)

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.680
Glitter bombs became
popular about 10 years ago

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.050
when some gay rights activists

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:04.220
started throwing glitter bombs
in the faces of politicians

00:01:04.220 --> 00:01:05.860
who opposed gay marriage.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.370
Some famous people have since
been on the receiving end

00:01:08.370 --> 00:01:09.203
of a glitter bomb.

00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:11.570
For example, in 2012, a
Boulder student was arrested

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:12.830
for glitter bombing Mitt Romney

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:14.230
at a Colorado campaign event.

00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.020
Prosecutors originally charged Peter Smith

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.070
with throwing a missile at Romney,

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:19.950
but he pleaded guilty
to disturbing the peace,

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:20.920
a lesser charge.

00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:23.490
Here while the glitter didn't
make much of a visual impact,

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:25.660
if you throw something at a politician,

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.440
you should probably expect the
Secret Service to tackle you.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:30.760
Lindsay Lohan was on the
receiving end of a glitter bomb

00:01:30.760 --> 00:01:32.020
while she was on her way to court.

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.670
As you can see, the use of the glitter

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.240
looked more like rice or confetti,

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.397
and I guess the message was,

00:01:36.397 --> 00:01:38.550
"Congratulations on your jail time."

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:40.180
Glitter bombing
celebrities and politicians

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.550
was all the rage for a couple
of years in the early aughts.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:44.880
This being America, the
glitter bomb fad encouraged

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.940
a few enterprising souls
to turn glitter bombing

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:47.920
into a business.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:49.380
Websites popped up all over the place

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:51.120
allowing you to send a
glitter bomb through the mail.

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:53.470
Just pay 39.99, enter in an address,

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:55.700
and presto, you've just pranked someone.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:58.300
So, at first glance, this all
just seems like harmless fun.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:01:59.900
Glitter is a pain in the ass to clean up,

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:01.690
but beyond that, what's the harm?

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.960
Amazon even sells something
called Unicorn Snot,

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.207
which is described as,
"Biodegradable holographic

00:02:06.207 --> 00:02:08.880
"body glitter gel for
body, face, and hair."

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:10.990
So it's safe to say the
glitter by itself is annoying,

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.650
but probably not dangerous.

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:14.950
But when we consider whether
any of this is criminal,

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.490
it depends on what you do with it and why.

00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.110
People have lots of different motivations

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:20.210
for sending a glitter bomb.

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.070
They may wanna do it as a prank, a joke,

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:23.460
or even for revenge.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:25.550
But in some cases, glitter can lead

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:27.620
to serious criminal charges.

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:30.350
It is alleged that the two
Florida women in question,

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:32.400
Kaitlin O'Donovan and Sarah Franks,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:33.900
went to Jacob Colon's apartment

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.850
at three o'clock in the morning

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.620
to argue with him about something.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:38.520
We don't know what the argument was about,

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.090
but while he stood on the balcony,

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:42.170
the two women through a
container of glitter at him,

00:02:42.170 --> 00:02:43.740
hitting his head and torso.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:46.830
Franks then scaled a fence,
broke into Colon's apartment,

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.350
and threw more glitter at Colon.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:50.240
When Colon was covered in glitter,

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.100
Franks let O'Donovan into the apartment

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:55.240
and she threw yet more
containers of glitter at Colon.

00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:57.330
Franks then kicked a
window until it broke,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.037
and then left the apartment.

00:02:59.037 --> 00:03:01.730
"The Smoking Gun" tracked
down the arrest affidavits,

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:03.590
which revealed that Colon
had called the police

00:03:03.590 --> 00:03:05.970
and gave them a description of their car.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:08.530
Police found the car
which was, unsurprisingly,

00:03:08.530 --> 00:03:09.990
covered in glitter.

00:03:09.990 --> 00:03:12.540
O'Donovan and Franks were
charged with felony burglary

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:14.370
and assault and battery.

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:16.920
Franks got an additional
charge for criminal mischief.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.430
So while the story is definitely more

00:03:18.430 --> 00:03:20.900
than just throwing glitter at someone,

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.560
the crux of the argument here

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:24.770
is that they went to someone's apartment

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.630
and threw glitter at them.

00:03:26.630 --> 00:03:28.510
And so to understand what's going on here,

00:03:28.510 --> 00:03:30.990
we have to talk about the
law of assault and battery.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:32.530
Assault and battery are criminal charges,

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:34.090
as well as civil torts.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:35.770
In the criminal context,
a person commits battery

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:38.980
if they intentionally cause a
harmful or offensive contact

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.340
with another person.

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:42.560
In many jurisdictions,
an assault is an attempt

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:43.393
to commit battery.

00:03:43.393 --> 00:03:45.110
In other words, any intentional act

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:46.930
that causes another person to fear

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.210
that he or she is about
to suffer physical harm

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:50.510
would be considered assault,

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:53.470
while the battery requires
actual physical conduct

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:56.930
by the person or an
extension of that person.

00:03:56.930 --> 00:03:58.540
The police report here
says that the two women

00:03:58.540 --> 00:04:01.970
hit Colon with a flying object,
multiple jars of glitter.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.670
If these facts are true,
that's potentially battery.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.060
And here the alleged facts suggest

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:07.650
that these acts were intentional

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.260
since the women apparently
rolled up on Colon's apartment

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.090
already armed with several
containers of glitter

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:13.940
and then, you know, covered his apartment

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:16.310
and his person with glitter.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:17.610
It might be hard for them to claim

00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:19.030
that they were just headed to his house

00:04:19.030 --> 00:04:19.863
in the middle of the night

00:04:19.863 --> 00:04:22.270
for an evening of hardcore crafting.

00:04:22.270 --> 00:04:24.350
And the police charged
both women with burglary,

00:04:24.350 --> 00:04:26.767
which in Florida is defined
as, "Entering a dwelling,

00:04:26.767 --> 00:04:28.397
"a structure, or a conveyance

00:04:28.397 --> 00:04:30.630
"with the intent to commit
an offense therein."

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:34.100
Okay, so it's not that
surprising that under these facts

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:36.000
you can be in big trouble
for breaking and entering

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.900
into someone's house,
assaulting them with glitter,

00:04:37.900 --> 00:04:39.100
and breaking a window.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.880
Still though, this is
not the kind of situation

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.500
where you would expect the perpetrators,

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.900
or the alleged perpetrators,
to be facing life in prison.

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:48.210
And there's definitely a
conversation to be had here

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:51.520
about the carceral state,
that we tend to only ratchet

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:54.430
the penalties for crimes up and not down,

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:56.990
and we tend to over-incarcerate people.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:00.230
This is a large and
systemic issue that we face.

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:02.140
But the thing that I
wanna talk about today

00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.680
is a little more lighthearted.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:06.370
What about all of these
other glitter attacks?

00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:08.730
How could they possibly be legal?

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:09.700
Well, to answer that question,

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.390
let's consider the legal implications

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.440
of Mark Rober's holiday
package glitter bombs.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.890
Because every December Mark
Rober spreads Christmas cheer

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:19.090
by getting back at package thieves.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:22.110
Honestly, these are some of my
favorite videos of the year.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:24.120
Rober engineer's a glitter
bomb inside of a box

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:25.550
that looks like an ordinary package.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.360
Once the porch thief picks up
the package and opens the box,

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:30.440
it detonates a glorious spray of glitter

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.960
and aerosolized farts.

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:35.310
This is NASA engineering at its finest.

00:05:35.310 --> 00:05:38.040
But so far, none of these
thieves have sued Rober,

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:40.410
nor has he been arrested.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:42.770
Doesn't his act, intentionally
planting a device

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.710
that explodes glitter in someone's face,

00:05:44.710 --> 00:05:47.100
meet the traditional
definition of battery?

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.970
Well, the answer might surprise you.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.090
And to dig into this,
let's look at the Texas law

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.040
since that's where Mark Rober's
original video took place.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.850
And we just talked about
assault when someone apprehends

00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.040
immediate bodily injury or contact,

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.830
and battery refers to the
actual bodily contact,

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.770
whether it's harmful or offensive.

00:06:03.770 --> 00:06:06.130
And the Texas statutes tend to mirror

00:06:06.130 --> 00:06:08.970
the generalized definition
of assault and battery.

00:06:08.970 --> 00:06:11.500
Under Texas law, intentionally
or knowingly causing

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.140
physical contact with another person

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.340
when that person should
know or reasonably believe

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:17.760
that the other will regard
the contact as offensive

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.110
or provocative is a misdemeanor crime,

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:22.280
unless committed against a public servant.

00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:25.070
So as with most things, we
have to start with intent,

00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:28.570
because intent is almost always
a requirement of the crime.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:32.480
And here did Rober knowingly
cause physical contact

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:33.890
with another person?

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:35.610
Well, it seems like it.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:36.900
He planted a device that he knew

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:38.320
would cause glitter particles to hit

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:40.860
or almost hit another
person or their property.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:42.250
And while these glitter
bombs haven't caused

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:44.830
any physical injury, I'm
gonna go out on a limb

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:48.160
and say that being covered
in glitter and fart spray

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.030
is offensive, or at least provocative.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.790
And if you think it's wrong for a thief

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:54.780
to have any human rights whatsoever

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:56.800
and to be able to complain about anything,

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:58.550
well, you should probably watch my video

00:06:58.550 --> 00:07:00.540
on the shotgun booby trap case,

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.220
which is based on the very famous case

00:07:02.220 --> 00:07:03.123
of Katko versus Briney.

00:07:03.123 --> 00:07:05.060
In that case, the Briney family owned

00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.890
a boarded-up farmhouse in Iowa.

00:07:06.890 --> 00:07:09.270
Marvin Katko and his accomplice
broke into the building

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:11.080
and stole old bottles and fruit jars.

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.850
The Brineys posted "No trespassing" signs,

00:07:12.850 --> 00:07:14.330
but the burglaries continued.

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:16.410
That's when Edward Briney
decided to take matters

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:17.250
into his own hands

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.700
and he mounted a 20-gauge
spring-loaded shotgun

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:21.630
in a farmhouse and rigged it to fire

00:07:21.630 --> 00:07:23.310
when the bedroom door was opened.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:25.860
The bedroom window was
also covered with steel.

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:29.320
In 1967, Katko and his accomplice
broke into the farmhouse,

00:07:29.320 --> 00:07:32.080
and this time Katko went into the bedroom,

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:34.900
tripping the trigger wire, the
gun fired into his right leg,

00:07:34.900 --> 00:07:36.560
blowing away most of his tibia.

00:07:36.560 --> 00:07:38.240
And while Katko was convicted of burglary,

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.410
after serving his time,
he sued the Briney family

00:07:40.410 --> 00:07:42.260
for assault and battery.

00:07:42.260 --> 00:07:45.160
In 1971, the Iowa Supreme
Court decided the case,

00:07:45.160 --> 00:07:47.220
and the Supreme Court
found that the rule of law

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:48.317
to be applied was this:

00:07:48.317 --> 00:07:50.667
"One may use reasonable
force to protect property

00:07:50.667 --> 00:07:53.217
"but may not use a means of
force that will take you in life

00:07:53.217 --> 00:07:55.227
"or inflict great bodily harm.

00:07:55.227 --> 00:07:56.827
"The fact that a trespasser may be acting

00:07:56.827 --> 00:07:58.817
"in violation of the law
would not change the rule,

00:07:58.817 --> 00:08:00.897
"and the only time the setting
of a spring gun is justified

00:08:00.897 --> 00:08:03.657
"is if a trespasser is
committing a felony of violence

00:08:03.657 --> 00:08:05.417
"or one punishable by death,

00:08:05.417 --> 00:08:07.137
"or where a trespasser is endangering

00:08:07.137 --> 00:08:08.710
"human life by his act."

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:12.660
And so a jury awarded Katko
$20,000 for actual damages,

00:08:12.660 --> 00:08:15.090
plus another $10,000 in punitive damages.

00:08:15.090 --> 00:08:16.370
And remember, this was the 1960s,

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.830
so that was quite a lot of money.

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.570
So it's probably an open and shut case

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:21.150
against Mark Rober, right?

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:22.690
Well, probably wrong.

00:08:22.690 --> 00:08:24.530
That's because unlike
a shotgun booby trap,

00:08:24.530 --> 00:08:26.330
a glitter bomb package isn't going to kill

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:27.970
or seriously injure anyone.

00:08:27.970 --> 00:08:29.700
Under Texas law, a person who was accused

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:31.920
of committing a battery
can avoid liability

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:33.270
if the act in question was committed

00:08:33.270 --> 00:08:34.870
in defense of their property,

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:37.950
if the act was reasonable and not lethal.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:40.450
Rober's glitter bomb is
not at all like a shotgun

00:08:40.450 --> 00:08:43.260
mounted to a bed set to kill an intruder.

00:08:43.260 --> 00:08:44.810
Under Texas self-defense law,

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:46.700
you're not permitted to use deadly force

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:47.950
to protect your property.

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:50.520
However, you can use
non-lethal and reasonable force

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.160
to protect your property
from thieves and trespassers.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.660
So it's a good thing the
glitter bomb is just glitter

00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:58.470
and fart spray because if he
sprayed the person with acid

00:08:58.470 --> 00:09:00.850
or bleach or rigged a
package to fire a weapon,

00:09:00.850 --> 00:09:03.890
explode, or release a pit of vipers,

00:09:03.890 --> 00:09:06.140
that probably would not be justified.

00:09:06.140 --> 00:09:07.960
It would also be a lot less entertaining

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:09.070
to watch on YouTube.

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:11.940
One might make an analogy
to anti-climb paint.

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:13.190
Anti-climb paint is slippery

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:15.080
and prevents someone from climbing a wall.

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:18.050
Now I'm only covering one of
the many potential defenses

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:19.140
for Mark Rober here.

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:21.280
There are, I think, a
bunch of others as well.

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:23.130
But the main thing is
that Mark didn't climb

00:09:23.130 --> 00:09:24.850
into some innocent person's house,

00:09:24.850 --> 00:09:27.540
break in, and start throwing
glitter and fart spray.

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.210
In the eyes of the law, Mark
Rober is not a criminal.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:32.406
At least about that.

00:09:32.406 --> 00:09:35.660
(suspenseful music)

00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:37.070
If only we could talk to Mark Rober

00:09:37.070 --> 00:09:38.660
to find out what he was thinking.

00:09:38.660 --> 00:09:40.150
- Okay, you gotta help me out here.

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:41.300
You gotta clear my name.

00:09:41.300 --> 00:09:44.000
Who else is gonna glitter
bomb these thieves?

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:46.654
- Mark, I told you not to use the snakes.

00:09:46.654 --> 00:09:47.990
(cheerful music)

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:50.970
All right, the man himself, Mark Rober.

00:09:50.970 --> 00:09:53.210
Thanks so much for joining today.

00:09:53.210 --> 00:09:54.490
- Good to be here.

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:56.270
Let's get to the bottom of this.

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:57.270
- Out of curiosity, at this point,

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:58.560
how much glitter and fart spray

00:09:58.560 --> 00:09:59.830
do you think you've gone through?

00:09:59.830 --> 00:10:01.700
- Like, what metric?

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:05.350
Like, by pounds or by
like number of boxes?

00:10:05.350 --> 00:10:07.840
- Well, I mean, you know,
you're a NASA engineer,

00:10:07.840 --> 00:10:11.960
so, you know, kilograms
probably more than pounds.

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:13.494
- We gotta stick with freedom unit.

00:10:13.494 --> 00:10:14.350
(Devin chuckling)

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:18.550
We're talking probably
like 44 pounds of glitter,

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:22.680
so that's like 20
kilograms, give or take, of,

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:26.190
don't quote me on that,
ish kilograms of glitter.

00:10:26.190 --> 00:10:29.760
And I would say probably we've
gotten like 50 boxes stolen

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:31.467
or something at this point.

00:10:31.467 --> 00:10:33.970
- And I know that you've gotten
law enforcement involved,

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:36.250
both in the porch thieves

00:10:36.250 --> 00:10:39.870
and on tracking down some
honest to goodness scammers

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.170
who were taking advantage of, you know,

00:10:42.170 --> 00:10:43.453
sometimes the elderly.

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:47.520
Has anything come from that coordination

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:48.750
with law enforcement?

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:52.920
- So we've literally had like
the Department of Justice,

00:10:52.920 --> 00:10:57.700
like FBI, Homeland Security,
Scotland Yard, Interpol,

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.190
like all of them at some point

00:10:59.190 --> 00:11:01.660
in some capacity have reached out,

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:05.080
including I think it was
like Microsoft's fraud team,

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:06.600
like companies as well.

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:08.460
We will just give them our information.

00:11:08.460 --> 00:11:09.457
We're just like, "Here you go.

00:11:09.457 --> 00:11:10.830
"Do with it what you want."

00:11:10.830 --> 00:11:13.030
They generally don't follow back with us

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:15.610
and say what happened,
for whatever reason.

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:17.100
It's not necessarily a two-way street.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:19.680
If we're looking with
like local jurisdictions,

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:23.020
like this year, we're gonna
work with San Francisco PD

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:26.110
and kind of tackle the car,

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:30.500
people smashing and grabbing
cars and like stuff from cars.

00:11:30.500 --> 00:11:32.140
So we're working more like that,

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:34.030
than there is more back and forth.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:37.560
But otherwise we just kind of
just give them all the data

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:38.820
and let them do with it what they will.

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:40.620
They have told us they
like what we're doing,

00:11:40.620 --> 00:11:43.020
A, because it's like
we do stuff technically

00:11:43.020 --> 00:11:45.880
that they just don't have the resources

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:47.230
or ability to do in their department.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:50.420
But B, we can get away with
stuff that they can't do

00:11:50.420 --> 00:11:52.780
because we're like
private citizens, I guess.

00:11:52.780 --> 00:11:55.170
But somehow they could
use that information.

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:57.717
So it's like in certain
cases, they be like,

00:11:57.717 --> 00:11:59.557
"No, we love what you're doing.

00:11:59.557 --> 00:12:00.640
"Please keep doing it."

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:02.480
For whatever reason,
they're either legally

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:05.370
or constrained from a resource
standpoint can't do it.

00:12:05.370 --> 00:12:08.610
So they love when we feed
them whatever we get.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:12.080
- Yeah, well, I mean,
that's a great example.

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:14.590
You know, the police are
bound by the Constitution.

00:12:14.590 --> 00:12:16.779
- Pfft! Lame!

00:12:16.779 --> 00:12:18.490
- (chuckles) Yeah, theoretically.

00:12:18.490 --> 00:12:20.690
Theoretically, they should
be bound by the Constitution,

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:21.837
and, you know, you get people saying,

00:12:21.837 --> 00:12:23.900
"Oh, that infringes on my
First amendment rights,"

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:25.990
when it doesn't actually
involve the government,

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:27.790
and the First Amendment
doesn't apply to anything

00:12:27.790 --> 00:12:28.870
but the government.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.810
You are a private citizen,
so there are rules

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:35.960
that define what the government
can do with information

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:37.780
that's created by private citizens.

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:40.530
Mark, I noticed that a lot
of the people in your videos

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:42.330
have blurred faces.

00:12:42.330 --> 00:12:46.000
Is there a particular
reason why you do that?

00:12:46.000 --> 00:12:47.550
- We might as well just
state it on your channel.

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:49.210
I put this in the description sometimes,

00:12:49.210 --> 00:12:52.550
but basically if someone
doesn't sign a release,

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:54.890
their face is then blurred.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:56.850
And I don't wanna ruin anyone's life

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:00.290
over like a stupid decision
to like steal a package.

00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:02.540
So by default, faces are blurred.

00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:04.450
If you see a face that's not blurred,

00:13:04.450 --> 00:13:05.950
that's because they signed a release

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:07.157
and we went back later and said,

00:13:07.157 --> 00:13:08.677
"Hey, will you sign a release

00:13:08.677 --> 00:13:10.250
"so I can put this in a dumb video?"

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:11.870
And they've signed it.

00:13:11.870 --> 00:13:16.020
- Are you able to prevent
future package thieves?

00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:18.320
- It's kind of a case by case basis.

00:13:18.320 --> 00:13:19.250
There's one two years ago,

00:13:19.250 --> 00:13:20.960
these two college students took it

00:13:20.960 --> 00:13:24.250
and they were hilarious.

00:13:24.250 --> 00:13:26.700
I didn't end up turning their footage

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:29.040
to the police or anything,
but then Discovery Channel...

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:29.873
We were doing something,

00:13:29.873 --> 00:13:31.560
so the Discovered
Channel tracked them down

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:34.247
and then I interviewed them,
and I asked them, like,

00:13:34.247 --> 00:13:36.530
"So have you guys stolen
any packages since?"

00:13:36.530 --> 00:13:39.407
And they're like, "Oh man,
I got in so much trouble

00:13:39.407 --> 00:13:41.410
"from my mom and my grandma."

00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:43.240
And then they're like,
you know, someone else,

00:13:43.240 --> 00:13:46.220
a package came to their
house accidentally,

00:13:46.220 --> 00:13:48.557
just shipped to the wrong
address, and he's like,

00:13:48.557 --> 00:13:51.867
"I frigging personally
walked that thing to Georgia

00:13:51.867 --> 00:13:55.180
"where it actually was
supposed to deliver it to."

00:13:55.180 --> 00:13:57.860
'Cause it's like, I didn't
want anything to do with this.

00:13:57.860 --> 00:13:59.657
He's like, "I'm just gonna
go through hell or high water

00:13:59.657 --> 00:14:02.050
"to make sure it got to the right person."

00:14:02.050 --> 00:14:05.670
So I was like, "Oh, well, my
job is done here," you know?

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:07.340
I think they learned their lesson.

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:10.590
- Well, Mark, if Batman
had a YouTube channel,

00:14:10.590 --> 00:14:15.260
I think he would aspire to
do exactly what you're doing.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:17.470
I think you're doing really good work.

00:14:17.470 --> 00:14:21.760
My final question for you is
if Mark Rober found himself

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:26.760
in jail, how would he engineer
his way out of imprisonment?

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:31.130
- (laughs) Oh man.

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:32.660
I mean, what kind of jail is it?

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.322
I gotta know like what
the weaknesses are, right?

00:14:35.322 --> 00:14:37.840
Usually those things come down

00:14:37.840 --> 00:14:41.250
to a little bit of like
combination of social engineering.

00:14:41.250 --> 00:14:44.070
You kind of got to observe,
know when the guards go on rest,

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:46.300
make friends with the guard, you know?

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:48.720
It depends on how long
the jail sentence was,

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:50.720
because also I'm not gonna like break out

00:14:50.720 --> 00:14:52.370
if that gives me like 20 more years.

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:54.210
So you've got to balance it all.

00:14:54.210 --> 00:14:55.460
But yeah--

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:58.440
- Let's say you're wrongfully
imprisoned for life.

00:14:58.440 --> 00:15:00.840
You are a completely innocent man,

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:05.230
but you have no hope of
parole and you're in Alcatraz.

00:15:05.230 --> 00:15:08.780
How does Mark Rober Mark
Rober his way out of Alcatraz?

00:15:08.780 --> 00:15:10.960
- Yeah, I'm going El Chapo, baby.

00:15:10.960 --> 00:15:12.430
I'm digging a tunnel.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:13.620
I'll invent a machine

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:16.120
that's like the fastest
digging tunnel machine,

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:17.530
and that's how I'm getting out, I think.

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:18.800
I'm calling it right now.

00:15:18.800 --> 00:15:20.810
- Maybe there's a
collaboration with Colin Furze

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:25.290
in digging a tunnel out of jail.

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:26.123
- Good point.

00:15:26.123 --> 00:15:28.730
I feel like I'm on Colin's
grass here on this one.

00:15:28.730 --> 00:15:31.230
All right, well, if I get
one phone call from jail,

00:15:31.230 --> 00:15:32.490
